                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The gameplay of ADAPTR is pretty straight-forward and easy to learn.  Your task is to build a robotic vehicle from a selecion of parts (various kinds of wheels, chassis blocks, joints, gadgets like a grabber, thrusters, lamps etc), suitable to finish the maps. And you need to modify your vehicle for each map as there are different kinds of obstacles. At first I was afraid that it doesn't offer much content for the price, having \"only\" 10 levels in the base game, and some of them are quite easy to \"finish\" for the first time. \n\nBut it turns out, this is the kind of game which is easy to play/finish, but damn hard to master. Some of the levels are already difficult when you only want to reach the goal point at the end of the map, and then there are additional achievements to master if you are going for a \"Gold\" rating instead of the \"Bronze\" medal you get for simply reaching the goal.  There's a par time to beat, a maximum component count, a target score, a hidden robot (ARTHER from the developer's other game Fortresscraft), and a max number of attempts per level if you really want that Gold medal.  So, even if you manage to breeze through the maps for the first time in a few hours, there's a lot of replay value for completionists. Or people trying to come up with exotic vehicle designs.  That said, I've already spent a considerable amount of time playing it, and still only have bronze medals for the first 9 levels, and didn't even beat the last level yet. \n\nThe graphics are good, as well as the soundtrack and sound effects. And the vehicle editor is very well done and intuitive to use. \n\nSo, overall, this is a nice and fun physics puzzler. Could be an interesting title for streamers to check out because the individual maps are relatively fast to finish, and you can get hilarious fails trying out, eh  \"suboptimal\" vehicle designs.  Might also be a cool game for speedrunners.  And completionists who want to 100% it.\n\nTherefore: Thumbs up!  Maybe not the best game of the year, but well worth it's money. I've had plenty fun with it so far and am still trying to finish the last level and get my first gold medal, so I can recommend it with good conscience. It's also really great how responsive the developer is:  I've stumbled on a few quirks/bugs myself, and after bringing them up on the games's Discord channel the developer instantly replied and fixed those issues on the same day! This is awesome \"customer service\" compared to some other games which have gamebreaking bugs for months or years and the devs don't seem to bother with fixing any of these.\n\nI wish there were a few more maps/levels, because some of them are pretty short and linear. But 12,50€ is still a fair price for what you get out of it, and hopefully there will be additional maps in the future (From the main menu, it looks like there are 2 additional chapters planned).",
